[Music]  hi it's Michele from lab love and beauty  science here chemistry PhD skin candor  and oxygen breather today we're going to  talk about oxygen masks how do they work  and what do they do apart from making  you look fluffy if you like this sort of  video please give it a thumbs up  subscribe to my channel and make sure  you click on the notification bell so  you don't miss any of my videos let's  talk about Archer now sure first up what  exactly is an oxygen mask oxygen masks  are products which come out of the  bottle like a gel but they start  bubbling with oxygen gas after you put  them on your skin for a bit as you wait  your skin gets covered in tickly bubbles  and you sort of start to look like a  cloud they're pretty darn entertaining  lots of brands have come out with oxygen  masks there's brands like bliss Peter  Thomas Roth dr. Brad Asik  Sephora and philosophy how exactly do  they get the oxygen into the bottle  these oxygen products work a lot like  soda water gas gets dissolved in the  water under pressure so for example  carbon dioxide gas is in soda water  this gas gets released when the pressure  drops so when you open the soda water  bottle  unlike carbon dioxide oxygen doesn't  really dissolve that well in water  instead you have solvents in these  products called perfluorocarbons there's  a stable unreactive carbon-based  compounds that have lots of fluorine on  them compared to water they can dissolve  about 20 times more oxygen and they've  been investigated for use in artificial  blood  these perfluorocarbons can be mixed with  the oxygen and then sealed in a  pressurized bottle so when it comes out  the oxygen will start fizzing out slowly  like a bubbly soft drink if you have a  thicker product you might not even need  a pressurized bottle the thickness of  the product can trap the oxygen inside  the oxygen gas only comes out when you  massage it over your skin it's a little  bit like pop rocks there are a lot of  different pet fluorocarbon products in  these oxygen masks the most common ones  are probably per Florida colon and  methyl per floor butyl ether but pretty  much anything in the ingredients list  with the word per floor in it will be a  puffer a carbon there are also some  oxygen masks that get oxygen from  hydrogen peroxide composing so for  example philosophise austrian peels are  like this you'll want to keep these  products away from dark fabrics because  peroxide will bleach them but the ones  that have perfluorocarbons will be safe  I'm using this bubble purifying and  charcoal face sheet mask the key  ingredients in this there are some  humectants so dye propylene glycol and  glycerine  there's also methyl per fluid butyl  ether methyl per floor acid butyl ether  and these two ingredients will dissolve  the oxygen and make it foam up again  when I put this on there are also some  other cool ingredients in this like  centella and a bunch of other fruit  extracts as well  you  this is massively sped up I forgot to  Robert to activate the bubbles before  putting it on and I got bored so I only  left it on for seven minutes instead of  10 to 15 if you want the full cloud  experience you should do both of these  things so what exactly is the point of  having oxygen in these products one of  the reasons is because the amount of  oxygen in your skin decreases as you get  older it also decreases if you smoke or  if you get a lot of UV exposure from the  Sun it's estimated that there's a  quarter less oxygen in your skin by the  time you're 30 and only half of it  remains by the time you're 40 you need  oxygen in your skin to produce collagen  so it's possible that if you don't have  enough oxygen then your collagen levels  will drop and you'll end up with thinner  saggy  duller skin and wrinkles there's been  some research that found that extra  oxygen can help with wound healing and  it increases something called the lag  Rijn this is a protein in your skin  that's important for keeping a healthy  skin barrier oxygen could also  potentially help kill acne bacteria  there was an animal study as well which  found that oxygen reduced wrinkles  caused by UV the problem with oxygen is  that there haven't been any convincing  studies done on humans about how it can  help your skin there's also a potential  side effect which is that the extra  oxygen could increase the amount of  reactive oxygen species or RS in your  skin these oxidants are important for  lots of biological processes but if you  have too many they can attack and damage  the proteins and DNA in your skin and  this leads to premature aging I've  talked about oxidative stress before in  my video on antioxidants and skincare a  few wound healing studies have looked at  whether this side effect is significant  and they found out it wasn't  [Music]  so now that we know that oxygen can be  good for your skin the question is is  there really enough in these products  that can get into your skin to make a  difference there are a few studies on  using oxygenated perfluorocarbons for  wound healing and there's also been one  very basic one on whether or not it can  reduce wrinkles but these studies were  all done using topical gels which are  things that you leave on your skin not  using wash off products like these masks  so in these studies there was probably  more oxygen getting into the skin most  studies that have been done on oxygen  and wound healing used other methods for  getting oxygen into the skin like oxygen  producing bandages rather than  perfluorocarbons the fact that you see  all these bubbles coming up when you use  an oxygen mask could mean that most of  the oxygen is going into the air and not  into your skin so in other words they're  not really getting to where they can  make a difference but the tickling  feeling does make a feel like it's doing  more the bubbling action could also  potentially loosen dirt so the product  can get deeper into your pore and then  the bubbles can help unclog it sort of  like how baking soda can unclog a drain  so I think overall until there's be more  studies done on these oxygen masks  they're mostly just a fun product that  might help you clean your face better  but I think that unlikely to have any  anti-aging benefits  I hope you like this video if you  enjoyed it please give it a thumbs up  and subscribe to my channel for more  videos like this you can also follow me  on Instagram and check out my blog for  more about beauty science let me know  what else you want me to talk about in  the comments and I'll see you next time

